-| Value added and gross output differ materially for this sector | §3 procedure on `highfill-2024-us-space-economy-statistics.md`, searching `131,776` and `232,061` | Either figure absent, or the two equal | Both present as BEA's own 2022 estimates: GDP (value added) `$131,776 million`, equal to `0.5 percent` of total US GDP; gross output `$232,061 million`. Two measures of one sector, one year, differing by roughly the size of the smaller. Deloitte names neither basis for its own total. |
-| The current global space economy has no single agreed size | §3 procedure on `oecd-2023-space-economy-in-figures.md`, searching the global estimate range | A single agreed figure | *"Estimates of the global space economy, excluding government procurement, range from about USD 200 billion to USD 350 billion depending on definition and method."* Deloitte's framing figure is *"more than US$613 billion generated annually,"* sourced to Space Foundation's *The Space Report 2025 Q2*. **These are not comparable and I am not claiming Deloitte's number is wrong** — the OECD range excludes government procurement and the Space Foundation figure does not. The finding is that the report cites one basis-free number to establish scale and never says which basis it is on, which is the same defect as the one in the row above. |
-| Helium-3 grade sets a mass-throughput floor the model does not carry | §3 procedure on `olson-2021-lunar-helium3-mining.md`, searching `ppb` and the M-3 excavation figures | The grade unstated, or stated only by attribution | The file's own reporting of Apollo 11 sample 10084: `11.8 ppb` mean, range `9.22` to `17.9 ppb`. M-3 miner concept: `1,258 tonnes/hour` excavation; roughly `33 kg` of helium-3 per year against *"nearly five million tonnes"* of regolith excavated at `10 ppb`. **Step 4 fired:** the file itself records that the paper does not reconcile its own two statements of that ratio, so this row establishes the order of magnitude and not a number. Deloitte's appendix contains no tonnage, no excavation rate and no grade, so this floor is absent from the model rather than disagreed with. |
-| The corpus holds a stated definition of economic self-sufficiency | §3 procedure on `metzger-2013-bootstrapping-space-industry.md`, searching `full closure` | No closure concept present | Present and central: a model of *"full material and electronic self-sufficiency ('full closure')"*, tracked generation by generation with an explicit electronics-import fraction per generation. Seeded at `12 t` to `41 t` landed over roughly 20 years, reaching `156 t` with 60 robots or `40,000 t` with 100,000 robots. **This is what an economic-sustainability variable looks like, and Deloitte has no analogue of it.** |
-| A prior paper made sustainability of presence conditional on commercial viability | §3 procedure on `downing-2005-sustainable-lunar-economy.md` | The paper treating presence and viability as independent | *"the sustainability of human lunar presence depends on developing a viable lunar economy based on profitable use of the Moon's resources."* Twenty-one years old, and the conditional is the one Deloitte's title asserts and its model does not test. |
